WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.348
The scheduling team supplies a mock API while the
real service is under construction.

00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:11.515
The dashboard team can now build a booking screen,
but an always-successful mock teaches the wrong

00:00:11.515 --> 00:00:12.270
assumptions.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:17.240
Include unavailable slots, invalid input and delayed
responses in the contract.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.204
Then run the same expectations against the real
implementation.

00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:27.680
A mock accelerates collaboration because it makes
assumptions executable; it does not prove that the

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.400
dependency behaves that way in production.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.840
The same scheduling API can serve a browser
dashboard and a technician’s mobile app.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.317
The browser may receive server-rendered HTML or
construct its interface from API responses; a hybrid

00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:43.418
is also possible.

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:48.682
In either case, the backend must enforce
authorization and validate requested changes.

00:00:48.682 --> 00:00:54.436
Hiding an unavailable action in a button does not
prevent a different client from sending that

00:00:54.436 --> 00:00:54.926
request.

00:00:54.926 --> 00:01:00.741
Review one complete workflow across both clients and
check that resource identities, validation

00:01:00.741 --> 00:01:05.699
failures and permission rules mean the same thing on
the shared service boundary.
